data: {"type":"handoff","handoff":{"text":"{\"v\":1,\"hash\":\"b9c4a57381ea9ddb699ee666755c58f30b5b91b61b304330fddf621f633a2b36\",\"at\":1794401559546,\"retried\":[],\"merged\":{\"byChunk\":[{\"chunkId\":1,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"1.1\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 (July 16–24, 1969) was the American spaceflight that first landed humans on the Moon and the fifth crewed Apollo mission.\"},{\"id\":\"1.2\",\"text\":\"The crew was Neil Armstrong (Commander), Michael Collins (Command Module Pilot), and Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin (Lunar Module Pilot), each on their second and final spaceflight.\"},{\"id\":\"1.3\",\"text\":\"The Saturn V launched from Kennedy Space Center on July 16, 1969 at 13:32 UTC.\"},{\"id\":\"1.4\",\"text\":\"The command module housed the three astronauts and was the only part to return to Earth; the service module supplied propulsion, power, oxygen, and water.\"},{\"id\":\"1.5\",\"text\":\"Armstrong and Aldrin landed the LM Eagle in the Sea of Tranquility on July 20 at 20:17 UTC while Collins remained in lunar orbit aboard Columbia.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin \\\"Buzz\\\" Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Command module (CM)\",\"Service module (SM)\",\"Lunar Module (LM)\",\"Eagle\",\"Columbia\"]},{\"chunkId\":2,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"2.1\",\"text\":\"Neil Armstrong walked on the Moon about six hours after landing, with Buzz Aldrin following nineteen minutes later.\"},{\"id\":\"2.2\",\"text\":\"The two spent about two and a half hours on the surface, planting a flag, deploying instruments, and collecting 21.5 kg of lunar material.\"},{\"id\":\"2.3\",\"text\":\"After over 21 hours on the surface, they rejoined Michael Collins in lunar orbit and returned to Earth, splashing down in the Pacific Ocean on July 24.\"},{\"id\":\"2.4\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 culminated the Space Race, a geopolitical contest between the United States and the Soviet Union rooted in Cold War rivalry.\"},{\"id\":\"2.5\",\"text\":\"President John F. Kennedy set the goal in May 1961 of landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the decade ended; the program overcame the fatal Apollo 1 fire in January 1967.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"Apollo 11\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 1\"]},{\"chunkId\":3,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"3.1\",\"text\":\"The Soviet Union could not match the Saturn V rocket, and its uncrewed probe Luna 15 crashed on the Moon on July 21.\"},{\"id\":\"3.2\",\"text\":\"Armstrong's moonwalk was broadcast live to an estimated 600 million viewers, roughly one-fifth of the world's population.\"},{\"id\":\"3.3\",\"text\":\"Stepping onto the lunar surface, Armstrong declared: \\\"That's one small step for [a] man, one giant leap for mankind.\\\"\"},{\"id\":\"3.4\",\"text\":\"Lunar samples from the mission led to identifying three previously unrecognized minerals, and surface instruments returned data for years.\"},{\"id\":\"3.5\",\"text\":\"The crew received ticker-tape parades, a world goodwill tour, and the Presidential Medal of Freedom. The command module Columbia is preserved at the National Air and Space Museum.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Luna 15\",\"Saturn V\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Columbia\",\"Eagle\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"United States\"]},{\"chunkId\":4,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"4.1\",\"text\":\"On October 4, 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, the first artificial satellite, surprising the world and fueling fears about Soviet capabilities.\"},{\"id\":\"4.2\",\"text\":\"Sputnik's success suggested the USSR could potentially deliver nuclear weapons over intercontinental distances, challenging American claims of superiority.\"},{\"id\":\"4.3\",\"text\":\"Sputnik sparked the Sputnik crisis and ignited the Space Race between the two superpowers.\"},{\"id\":\"4.4\",\"text\":\"President Eisenhower responded by creating NASA and initiating Project Mercury, which aimed to place a man into Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"4.5\",\"text\":\"On April 12, 1961, Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space and first to orbit the Earth; Alan Shepard followed on May 5, 1961 with a suborbital flight.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Space Race\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"John F. Kennedy\"]},{\"chunkId\":5,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"5.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy, Eisenhower's successor, chose a challenge beyond existing rocket capabilities so both countries would start from an equal position.\"},{\"id\":\"5.2\",\"text\":\"A crewed Moon mission was presented as the challenge that would serve this purpose.\"},{\"id\":\"5.3\",\"text\":\"On May 25, 1961, Kennedy told Congress the nation should commit to landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the 1960s ended.\"},{\"id\":\"5.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said the project would be more impressive to mankind and more important for long-range space exploration, but also very difficult and expensive.\"},{\"id\":\"5.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed accelerating lunar spacecraft development, developing alternate liquid and solid fuel boosters, and funding engine development and unmanned exploration.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\"]},{\"chunkId\":6,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"6.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said that sending a man to the Moon would be a national effort, since all Americans must work to put him there.\"},{\"id\":\"6.2\",\"text\":\"On September 12, 1962, Kennedy gave a speech to about 40,000 people at Rice University's football stadium in Houston, Texas.\"},{\"id\":\"6.3\",\"text\":\"Kennedy argued that space has no strife, prejudice, or national conflict yet, and its hazards threaten everyone alike.\"},{\"id\":\"6.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said the conquest of space deserves the best of all mankind and its chance for peaceful cooperation may never return.\"},{\"id\":\"6.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy answered 'why the Moon?' by comparing it to climbing mountains, flying the Atlantic, and Rice playing Texas, concluding 'We choose to go to the Moon.'\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Moon\",\"Atlantic\",\"Texas\"]},{\"chunkId\":7,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"7.1\",\"text\":\"The chunk quotes a stated choice to go to the Moon this decade because the challenge is hard, not easy.\"},{\"id\":\"7.2\",\"text\":\"The proposed Moon program faced widespread opposition; mathematician Norbert Wiener dubbed it a \\\"moondoggle.\\\"\"},{\"id\":\"7.3\",\"text\":\"The effort to land a man on the Moon was already named Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"7.4\",\"text\":\"In June 1961, Kennedy proposed a joint Moon landing to Soviet Premier Nikita Khrushchev, who declined.\"},{\"id\":\"7.5\",\"text\":\"After Kennedy's death, the idea of a joint Moon mission was abandoned; lunar orbit rendezvous was chosen over direct ascent and Earth orbit rendezvous.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"Soviet Union\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"Moon\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\"]},{\"chunkId\":8,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"8.1\",\"text\":\"A space rendezvous is an orbital maneuver in which two spacecraft navigate through space and meet up.\"},{\"id\":\"8.2\",\"text\":\"In July 1962, NASA head James Webb announced that lunar orbit rendezvous would be used for Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"8.3\",\"text\":\"The command module had a cabin for three astronauts and was the only part that returned to Earth; the service module supplied propulsion, power, oxygen, and water.\"},{\"id\":\"8.4\",\"text\":\"The lunar module had a descent stage for landing on the Moon and an ascent stage to return astronauts to lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"8.5\",\"text\":\"This design let a single Saturn V rocket, then under development, launch the spacecraft; Apollo enabled by MOSFETs in the IMP and silicon ICs in the AGC.\"}],\"entities\":[\"James Webb\",\"NASA\",\"Apollo\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Saturn V\",\"Command module\",\"Service module\",\"Lunar module\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer\"]},{\"chunkId\":9,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"9.1\",\"text\":\"The Apollo 1 fire on January 27, 1967 killed astronauts Gus Grissom, Ed White, and Roger B. Chaffee and abruptly halted Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"9.2\",\"text\":\"Apollo 7 in October 1968 evaluated the command module in Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.3\",\"text\":\"Apollo 8 in December 1968 tested the command module in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.4\",\"text\":\"Apollo 9 in March 1969 tested the lunar module in Earth orbit, and Apollo 10 in May 1969 conducted a 'dress rehearsal' in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.5\",\"text\":\"By July 1969, everything was ready for Apollo 11 to make the final step onto the Moon.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Project Apollo\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\",\"Apollo 11\"]}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Edwin \\\"Buzz\\\" Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Command module (CM)\",\"Service module (SM)\",\"Lunar Module (LM)\",\"Eagle\",\"Columbia\",\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Luna 15\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Presidential Medal of Freedom\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Moon\",\"Earth\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Atlantic\",\"Texas\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"James Webb\",\"Apollo\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Command module\",\"Service module\",\"Lunar module\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\"],\"findingCount\":9}}","seal":"d1fc30cedbdebb2316c577dc8d2bcb2ba9e29d11f337722727589d9babb9cb1d"}}

